Yeah, the I Am Psyched exhibit is one of the most wonderful things I have been a part of in my career. It was launched in 2016 as part of the Smithsonian Institution's Museum Day, which they hold every year. So I worked with the American Psychological Association with Dr. Sherry Miles Cohen, as well as Dr. Alexander Rutherford, who runs a project called Psychology's Feminist Voices. And together we decided to create a traveling exhibit that could go from site to site that would showcase women of color in the history of psychology. And we worked with archival materials from our collections and created this exhibit that shared women of color in psychology all the way from the early 1900s to the first African-American woman to get a PhD in psychology all the way through to the present looking at, for example, the work of Jennifer Eberhardt, who has done such groundbaking work on stereotyping and racial prejudice. And this exhibit was so successful. I was expecting it to be great, and I was expecting it to gain some ground. But really, I mean, at this point, it's traveled, I think, to more than 40
是的，“I Am Psyched”展览是我职业生涯中参与过的最棒的事情之一。它于2016年启动，作为史密森尼学会每年举办的博物馆日的一部分。所以我与美国心理学协会的雪莉·迈尔斯·科恩（Sherry Miles Cohen）博士，以及负责一个名为“心理学的女性主义声音”（Psychology's Feminist Voices）项目的亚历山大·卢瑟福（Alexander Rutherford）博士合作。我们共同决定创建一个可以从一个地方巡展到另一个地方的巡回展览，展示心理学历史上的有色人种女性。我们使用了来自我们藏品的档案材料，并创建了这个展览，分享了从20世纪初到获得心理学博士学位的第一个非裔美国女性，一直到现在的心理学有色人种女性，例如，着眼于詹妮弗·埃伯哈特（Jennifer Eberhardt）的工作，她在刻板印象和种族偏见方面做了如此开创性的工作。这个展览非常成功。我原本期望它会很棒，并且我期望它能取得一些进展。但真的，我的意思是，到目前为止，它已经巡展了，我想，超过40个

