sites across the country, all the way from the East Coast to the West Coast. But the more exciting thing about it is that as the exhibit goes up in any given site, and we hosted it here at the Cunning Center, people often will also have an event around it, so they'll get together a panel of speakers. They'll have a party. They'll get students to serve as docents for the exhibit. And because of this, it just brings all these people together in this very powerful celebratory way to share and think about and talk about women of color in psychology's past and present. And it's been wonderful. I think that it has created change already. And I think that moving forward, we can continue to sort of mobilize that and help young girls and young women of
